<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José Cuero y Caicedo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1735">1735</a>–<a href="/years/1815">1815</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as José de Cuero y Caicedo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José Cuero y Caicedo was a churchman born in Cali who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> at the start of the 19th century. He supported the Quito autonomy movement and presided over the second junta and the State of Quito from 1811. After the royalist reconquest in 1812 he was sent into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, where he died.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-cuero-y-caicedo.jpg" alt="José Cuero y Caicedo" width="320" height="609" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous / Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jos%C3%A9_de_Cuero_y_Caicedo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
